==English==

===Etymology===
From {{suf|en|poet|ess}}. Compare {{cog|enm|poetresse|t=poetess}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˌpəʊ.ɪˈtɛs/}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ˈpoʊ.ɪˌtɛs/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|dated}} A [[female]] [[poet]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1830|title=Juvenilia|chapter=Leonine Elegiacs|chapterurl={{fullurl:s:en:Leonine Elegiacs}}|author=[[w:Alfred, Lord Tennyson|Alfred Tennyson]]|passage=The ancient '''poetess''' singeth, that Hesperus all things bringeth, / Smoothing the wearied mind: bring me my love, Rosalind.}}

====Usage notes====
{{m|en|poetess|Poetess}} is rare in contemporary usage according to which both sexes are known normally as {{m|en|poet|poets}}. The Latin term for a female poet was {{m|la|poētria}}, derived from the {{cog|grc|ποιήτρια}}, which still means “female poet” in Modern Greek. “Female poet” is the more usual modern translation where the gendered form in the original language is preserved.

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|poetress}} {{q|obsolete}}

